insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('clearing-out-their-things', 'Clearing Out Their Things', 'The wardrobe, the shed, the handwriting on a shopping list. Why sorting a person''s belongings is harder than anyone warns you, and how to do it at a pace you can bear.', 'You have opened the wardrobe four times and closed it again. There is a coat in there that still smells of them, and a shopping list in their handwriting on the kitchen counter that you cannot throw away.

Everyone treats this as a practical job. It is not a practical job. It is one of the hardest parts of losing someone, and almost nobody is warned about it.

Why objects hit so hard

Grief does not arrive on schedule; it arrives through the senses.

A coat holds a smell. A mug has the chip they made. A diary has next week''s appointments in it, written by someone who expected to be there. Those details land in a way that a conversation about the funeral never does, because they are evidence of an ordinary life that was still going.

And the small things are often worse than the big ones. People brace for the photographs and are undone by a half-used tube of hand cream.

There is no deadline, except the ones that are real

Most of this can wait. If the house does not have to be cleared, there is no reason at all to do it by a particular date, and doing it too early is something people regret more often than doing it late.

Where there is a real deadline — a rented property, a care home room, a sale — find out exactly what it is and how much flexibility exists, because the panic in your head is usually tighter than the actual terms.

If the room has to be emptied fast, you do not have to sort as you go. Box it, label it, store it, and do the deciding months later when you can. That is not avoidance; it is separating two jobs that do not have to happen at once.

Do it in short shifts, with someone there

Two hours is plenty. Four will flatten you for days.

Have someone with you if you can, and choose them carefully: not the person who wants to be efficient, but the one who can sit while you hold a jumper and say nothing. Their job is to be there, make tea, and carry boxes to the car.

Eat beforehand, drink water, and plan something gentle afterward. Do not schedule anything that needs you to be competent for the rest of the day.

And agree a signal for stopping, out loud, in advance: when I say I am done, we stop. Then stop.

Start with the easy shelf

Work from the least loaded thing toward the most, not in the order the room suggests.

Cleaning products, old paperwork, the contents of the bathroom cabinet, food in the cupboards. Those can mostly go without much feeling, and getting through them builds a little momentum.

Leave for last the things you already know are difficult: the clothes, the handwriting, the letters, the toolbox, the handbag. And give yourself permission to leave one drawer closed for a year.

Keep less than you want to, and more than feels sensible

People swerve into one of two ditches: keeping everything, which turns a garage into a shrine nobody can face, or clearing it all in a week and grieving the loss twice.

A middle path works better. Keep a small number of things, on purpose, and choose them for use rather than for storage: the mug you drink from, the jumper you wear, the tools in your shed, one picture out where you can see it.

Photograph what you are letting go of, if that helps. Many people find the picture holds the memory perfectly well once the object is gone.

And one or two things can simply be kept for no reason at all. A shopping list in their handwriting is allowed to live in a drawer for the rest of your life.

Giving things away is easier when you know where they go

It is much less painful to pass something to a person or a place than to a skip.

A friend who would wear the coat. The charity shop they used. Tools to someone starting out. Books to the library. A quilt or a cushion made from a shirt, if that is the sort of thing you like.

Children and grandchildren often want something small and specific rather than furniture. Asking them is usually better than guessing.

When other people are involved

Families fall out over objects, and it is almost never really about the objects.

Say the real thing where you can: I am not fighting you for the clock, I am frightened of having nothing left of him. That sentence prevents more arguments than any fair-division system.

Practically: agree the few items that matter to each person first, take turns rather than negotiating item by item, keep anything contested in a box rather than deciding while everyone is raw, and let somebody outside the family help if it has already gone wrong.

When to get some help with this

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if months have passed and you cannot enter the room at all, if the house has become impossible to live in because nothing can be moved, or if the grief has not softened at all with time and is keeping you from working, eating or sleeping.

Grief counseling exists for exactly this, and bereavement services will often see you without a referral; clearing a house is one of the most common things people bring, and nobody there will think you are being sentimental. A therapist can also help with the guilt that tends to come with it, which is nearly universal and almost never deserved.

And if the loss has taken you somewhere dark, and you are not sure you want to be here, please tell a doctor or someone you trust today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Loss', 'Self-Compassion']::text[], 0, '2026-10-13T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('panic-on-the-highway', 'Panic on the Highway', 'The lane goes on, there is no exit, and your body decides this is an emergency. What is happening when panic arrives in the car, and how to get your driving back.', 'You are in the middle lane, the traffic is heavy, and the next exit is six miles away. Your hands go cold, the road seems to tilt, and the thought arrives: I cannot get out of here.

After that it tends to shrink. First you avoid that stretch, then the highway, then anything with a bridge or a tunnel, and eventually the route to work takes forty minutes longer than it used to.

Why the car is such a common place for this

Panic attaches itself to places you cannot easily leave, and a highway is the clearest example there is.

No shoulder to stop on. No exit for miles. Speed you cannot slow down without consequence. Nobody to help. And a job you must keep doing with your hands and feet while your body behaves as though something is terribly wrong.

The monotony contributes too. Long, straight, unchanging road with little to look at can produce a drifting, slightly unreal feeling, which your alarm system then reads as a symptom rather than as boredom.

What is actually happening in your body

Adrenaline, and nothing more dangerous than that.

Fast heart, tight chest, shallow breathing, tingling hands, legs that feel distant, vision that seems narrow or too bright. Those are alarm, and they peak and come down; panic cannot keep itself going indefinitely, and it does not cause you to lose control of the car.

The frightening part is usually not the road at all. It is the sensations plus the thought about what the sensations mean, which is why the same stretch of highway can be fine on Tuesday and terrible on Thursday.

What to do in the moment

Keep it very simple, because this is not the time to learn anything new.

Loosen your grip and let your shoulders drop. Breathe out longer than you breathe in, two or three times, and do not take big gulps of air, which makes the tingling worse.

Put your eyes where they belong: far down the road, not on your own chest. Narrow attention onto your body makes everything louder, and looking at the horizon is both calming and good driving.

Say something flat and true out loud: this is adrenaline, it peaks in a couple of minutes, I can drive while it does. Then slow slightly and move to the slower lane, which is the small amount of control you actually have.

Open the window a little, or turn the fan to your face. Cool air on your skin does something real.

Plan your stop before you need it

Most of the fear is about having no exit, so give yourself exits.

Know where the next rest stop or service area is, and give yourself permission to use it. Pulling in for five minutes is a reasonable thing to do and it is not an admission of anything; standing up next to the car for two minutes resets a surprising amount.

If it happens often, drive the route at a quiet hour first so you know where the gaps are.

Do not let the avoidance set

This is the part that decides how the next six months go.

Every time you take the long way round, you get relief, and the relief teaches your alarm that the highway was genuinely dangerous and that avoiding it was the rescue. The fear grows on that, which is why routes shrink.

So go back, in small doses, and sooner than you want to. One junction with someone beside you. Then two alone. Then the same stretch again the next day, and again, until it is boring rather than until you feel confident.

Keep the drives short and frequent rather than long and brave. Repetition is what teaches the lesson; one heroic trip followed by three weeks off does not.

And drop the props slowly: the passenger who has to be there, the bottle of water you clutch, the tablet in your pocket, the route that stays within a mile of home. Each one quietly tells your alarm you are still in danger.

Make driving conditions easier while you work on it

Sleep matters more than people think; a short night raises your resting arousal and makes everything feel closer to the edge.

Go easy on caffeine before a long drive, eat something so you are not driving on empty, and get your eyes and your ears checked if you have not lately, because poor vision at speed is a real and treatable cause of feeling unsafe.

Music or a podcast you know well helps most people. Silence leaves room for the monitoring.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a doctor if the symptoms do not fit this pattern — if you have fainted, had chest pain under exertion, or felt genuinely confused — because that deserves checking before anything else.

And talk to a doctor or a therapist if panic in the car is shaping your life: if you have given up driving, turned down work, or stopped visiting people because of the roads in between. Panic responds very well to treatment, usually in a small number of sessions, and the method is largely what is above, done in order with someone keeping you honest about the avoidance. A driving instructor who works with anxious drivers is also a genuinely good use of money.

And if it has become dark enough that you are not sure you want to be here, please tell a doctor or someone you trust today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Panic', 'Grounding']::text[], 0, '2026-10-13T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-someone-does-not-reply', 'When Someone Does Not Reply', 'You sent it two days ago and nothing has come back. Why silence is so easy to read badly, what it usually means, and what to do instead of composing the follow-up again.', 'You sent the message on Sunday. It is Tuesday night. You have checked whether it was delivered, reread what you wrote, and noticed they have posted something since.

By now you have a theory, and the theory is about you.

Silence has no content, so you supply it

A message that has not been answered contains no information at all, which is exactly why it is so uncomfortable. Your mind does not tolerate a blank; it fills it.

And it fills it with your own particular fear. Some people conclude they have been rude, others that they are boring, others that the friendship was always one-sided. The silence is identical in every case; the explanation comes from you rather than from them.

Then the rereading starts, which is the stage that does the real damage. Read anything you wrote eight times and it will start to look strange, needy or badly judged, whatever it actually said.

What it usually turns out to be

When people are asked, years of answers come back and almost none of them are what the sender feared.

I read it in bed and meant to reply properly. I was waiting until I had time to answer the question in it. I forgot. I was having a bad week. I thought I had replied. The notification got buried. I am behind on everything, not just you.

Posting a photograph while leaving a message unanswered is not evidence either; those take entirely different amounts of effort, and people reliably do the easy one first.

That does not make the silence pleasant. It does mean the confident version in your head is probably wrong.

Check the thought the way you would check anything else

Write the prediction down plainly: she has decided I am too much and is going to drift away.

Then ask what the actual evidence is. What has she done over the last year? Has this happened before, and what did it turn out to be? What else would explain two days of nothing from someone with two small children and a job?

Then ask the question that cuts through most of it: if a friend told me they had not had a reply for two days, what would I say to them?

You would say people are busy. The standard you apply to yourself is not the one you apply to anyone else.

Do not send the second message yet

The follow-up that arrives at ten at night, apologizing for the first one, is the thing most likely to make an ordinary silence awkward.

So hold off. Give it a normal amount of time, which for most people and most messages is several days rather than several hours. Then, if you want to, send something short and warm with no audit in it: thinking of you, how did the week go?

Never send the apology version. Sorry for bothering you asks them to manage your feelings as well as answer the question, and it rewrites a normal exchange as something that needed forgiving.

And if you genuinely need an answer, ask for the thing rather than for reassurance: are you still free on Friday, or shall I make other plans?

Stop the checking

The behaviors around the silence keep it alive: looking to see whether it was read, checking when they were last online, watching what they post, rereading your own words, drafting replies you do not send.

Each check gives a few seconds of relief and leaves the question wider open than before. Put the phone in another room and do something that uses your hands for half an hour; the urge fades much faster than it feels like it will.

If it is a pattern, look at the pattern

Some of this is not about one message. If every unanswered text turns into a day of dread, the sensitivity is the thing worth working on rather than the individual silence.

Being quickly certain you have been dropped usually comes from somewhere: a childhood where approval moved around, a friendship that ended without explanation, a relationship where you had to read the weather constantly. That is understandable and it is also workable.

A practical step in the meantime: widen the circle. When one person''s reply carries the whole weight of whether you are liked, every delay is enormous. Three or four ordinary, low-stakes connections make any single silence much smaller.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a therapist or a counselor if waiting for replies is taking up large parts of your day, if the fear of being dropped is keeping you from reaching out at all, or if you find yourself testing people to see whether they still care.

This is one of the things therapy is unusually good at: the pattern is clear, the steps are practical, and most people notice a difference within a few sessions. And if the loneliness underneath it has flattened your mood for weeks rather than days, mention that to a doctor too, because it is common, treatable, and much easier to work on than it feels while you are waiting for a message.', array['Social Anxiety', 'Thinking Traps']::text[], 0, '2026-10-13T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-you-cannot-get-comfortable-in-your-own-skin', 'When You Cannot Get Comfortable in Your Own Skin', 'Jumpy, restless, unable to settle to anything, and sitting still makes it worse. What that agitated feeling usually is, and why moving works better than trying to relax.', 'You cannot sit down. You have started three things and finished none of them, you are irritable with everyone, and your legs want to be somewhere else. Lying down is worse. So is the television.

It is not quite anxiety and not quite anger. It is the feeling of being wired, and the usual advice to relax is useless, because relaxing is exactly what you cannot do.

What the restlessness usually is

Energy that has been mobilized and has nowhere to go.

Your body prepares for action under pressure: heart rate up, muscles ready, attention scanning. If the pressure is a difficult email rather than something physical, none of that gets used, and you are left with a system revved up and parked.

Common feeders, often several at once. A tense day with no movement in it. Too much caffeine. Short sleep, which makes almost everyone jumpy by mid-afternoon. A conversation you are dreading, or one you have just had. Alcohol wearing off. Too many hours on a screen. And for some people, a change in medication or a dose, which is worth mentioning to a doctor rather than pushing through.

Why trying to relax backfires

Sitting down and attempting to be calm asks an activated body to go from ten to one in one step, and it does not work that way.

Worse, when you sit still with nothing to do, your attention goes inward, notices the restlessness, and treats it as a problem to be solved. That adds a layer of frustration to the original agitation.

The ladder has a middle. You come down through movement, not straight to stillness, which is why a walk settles people when a meditation cushion sometimes does not.

Use up the energy first

Do something physical, briefly and without turning it into a project.

A fast ten-minute walk, preferably outside. Stairs. A few sets of something heavy. Scrubbing a floor, digging, carrying the shopping, cycling to the shop. Shaking your hands and arms out hard for thirty seconds, which looks ridiculous and works.

Push against something with real effort for ten seconds, then let go. Press your palms together, push a wall, squeeze a cushion. Tension followed by release gives the body the completion it was waiting for.

The aim is not fitness. It is to let the system finish the thing it started.

Then come down through something rhythmic

Once the edge is off, rhythm settles the rest.

Walking at an easy pace, swimming, kneading dough, sweeping, folding laundry, knitting, playing an instrument badly. Anything repetitive with a bit of physical involvement.

And let your breathing lengthen on its own as you go, rather than making breathing the task. Longer out-breaths than in-breaths will arrive by themselves once your body has moved.

Give your attention something to hold

Agitation is partly a lack of anywhere to put your attention, so hand it something real.

Feel your feet hitting the ground, one, two. Name five things you can see, four you can hear. Hold something cold. Notice the temperature of the air on your face. These are not tricks to feel better; they are places for attention to rest that are not the inside of your chest.

If you want to sit with it rather than move, make it short and make it kind: two minutes, with your feet on the floor, letting the buzzing be there without trying to flatten it. Restlessness tolerated usually fades faster than restlessness fought.

Take the inputs down

Afterward, make the next few hours easier rather than harder.

Caffeine has a long tail; something at four in the afternoon is still working at ten. Alcohol settles you for an hour and then raises your arousal in the second half of the night. Screens and news keep the alarm topped up.

Eat something if it has been hours, because low fuel and jitteriness are hard to tell apart. Get outside in daylight if you can; it does more for the wired feeling than most people expect.

Look for the thing underneath

Restlessness that keeps returning is usually pointing at something.

An unsaid sentence. A decision you are avoiding. A deadline. Anger you have not admitted to. Something you need to leave, or start.

So ask plainly: what am I not doing, or not saying? Quite often the agitation drops noticeably as soon as the real thing is named, even before anything is done about it. Writing for ten minutes with a pen is a good way to find it.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a doctor if this has been constant for weeks rather than coming and going, if it arrived with a new medication or a change in dose, if it comes with a racing heart, weight changes, tremor or heavy sweating, or if you cannot sit still at all. Thyroid trouble, some prescriptions and withdrawal from alcohol can all produce exactly this, and all are worth checking rather than enduring.

And talk to a doctor or a therapist if the restlessness is coming with a low mood, with thoughts moving unusually fast, or with sleep that has broken down. Those are treatable, the help is practical, and being wired and unable to settle is a perfectly good reason to make an appointment.', array['Movement', 'Mindfulness']::text[], 0, '2026-10-13T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('the-long-empty-sunday', 'The Long Empty Sunday', 'The day with nothing in it and nobody to speak to, stretching from breakfast until dark. Why Sundays are the hardest day for so many people, and how to make one bearable.', 'It is eleven in the morning and the whole day is in front of you. You have had no reason to speak out loud to anyone. The shops are quieter, everyone else appears to be having lunch with people, and by four o''clock the silence has weight.

If Sundays are the day you dread, you are in enormous company, and nobody says it out loud because it sounds like an admission.

Why this particular day is hard

Several things land together.

Structure disappears. Weekdays have shape even when they are dull; a Sunday gives you nothing to lean on, and an unstructured day is far harder than a busy one when your mood is already low.

Everything is closed or paired up. The places you might go are shut or full of families, and the cultural picture of a Sunday is lunch with people, which makes being on your own feel like a verdict rather than a circumstance.

There is nowhere to put the thinking. With no tasks, attention turns inward, and what it finds is usually a review of your life rather than a plan for the afternoon.

And tomorrow is coming, which adds its own dread on top.

Give the day a shape before it arrives

The single most useful thing is deciding on Saturday what Sunday contains. Not a full timetable; three fixed points.

Something in the morning that gets you out of the house, even for twenty minutes. Something in the middle with other humans somewhere in it, even strangers. Something in the evening to look forward to, however small.

Write them down. An intention held in your head at nine in the morning does not survive contact with a flat mood; a list on the counter often does.

And get dressed and leave the house once before midday, early, before the day sets. The difference this makes is out of all proportion to the effort.

Being among people is not the same as socializing

On a day when you have nobody to see, incidental company still counts for a surprising amount.

A cafe where you sit in rather than take away. A library. A market. A swimming pool. A church service if that is in your world. Walking where other people walk rather than where it is empty. A gym class where nobody talks to you but you are in a room with others.

Nobody has to speak to you for this to work. Being around other people takes an edge off the silence that four more hours indoors never will.

And if you can get one actual exchange into the day — the person at the till, a neighbor, a dog walker — the day usually turns slightly.

Put something with people in it on next Sunday

The lasting fix is to make Sundays a day with something fixed in them, and that takes a few weeks to build.

Things that reliably work: a volunteer shift, a choir, a walking group, a sports club, a regular class, a community meal, a standing call with someone at the same time each week.

Volunteering deserves a special mention, because it provides company, structure, and the feeling of being useful all at once, and it does not require you to be good at small talk.

Invite rather than wait, too. Most people are also at home on a Sunday afternoon and would come for a walk if asked; almost nobody initiates. A standing arrangement beats a hopeful text, because it removes the asking.

What to do with the thoughts

On a quiet day the mind will offer you a summary of your whole life, and it will not be an accurate one.

Everyone else has someone. This is how it will always be. There is something wrong with me. That is a low mood talking, and it gets louder in the empty hours and quieter after a walk or a conversation.

You do not have to argue with it. Notice it, name it as the day rather than the truth — this is Sunday afternoon, not my life — and go and do the next small thing on the list.

The screen is the trap

Scrolling other people''s weekends on a bad Sunday is the most reliable way to make the day worse, because you are comparing your quiet afternoon with the best ten seconds of everybody else''s.

So leave the phone in a drawer for a few hours and keep the comforts real and physical. A bath, a dish you like cooking, something with your hands, a walk, a familiar film rather than an endless feed.

When to get help

Please talk to a doctor if the low mood is not lifting between Sundays, if you are sleeping badly or not eating properly, or if the dread has spread across more and more of the week.

Loneliness is not a weakness and it is not a personality flaw; it is a signal, like hunger, that something you need is missing, and it responds to practical change more than people expect. A doctor can also point you toward the local things that exist for exactly this, including social prescribing and groups you would never find on your own, and a therapist is a good place to take the harder question of why connection has become difficult.

And if a Sunday has taken you somewhere dark, and you are not sure you want to be here, please tell a doctor or someone you trust today rather than waiting for Monday. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Loneliness', 'Depression']::text[], 0, '2026-10-13T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
